y holds the Raft instance can shut the node down, because Shutdown closes the shutdown channel that the blocked send also selects on, which makes the batch respond ErrRaftShutdown and releases the main loop. That is precisely the escape the startup-replay finding lacks: there the same send blocks before NewRaft has returned, so no caller can hold a handle to close that channel, and the only reader is a goroutine the same constructor starts after the blocking call. The distinction is worth recording for the report because it is what makes the two cases different in kind rather than in degree, and it is drawn from the two anchors rather than from a run. The behavioural half of this question - whether a stalled main loop is long enough to matter, in that it stops processing RPCs and its own lease check while commits continue - still needs the harness described in the discriminator, so the claim here stays unresolved rather than asserted.\\\",\n  \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"ab0cc02efafd4a8ab13a9d1118e251e9-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\n   \\\"assertion\\\":\\\"The entry says the library transfers the FSM's latency into the main loop by a blocking send with no rejection path; this reading adds that the same select has a shutdown arm, so a caller holding the handle can release it.\\\"}],\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"The shutdown arm bounds the stall only for a caller that holds the handle and chooses to stop the node; it is not a repair for the cluster, which loses that node.\\\",\n   \\\"The FSM's own progress is the only other bound, and it depends entirely on the application, which is why the question's behavioural half cannot be settled by reading.\\\",\n   \\\"No measurement was made for this question, so nothing here says how long a stall lasts in practice or how it interacts with the leader's lease in a real cluster.\\\"],\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"This is a source-only part-answer that leaves the question open rather than a finding.\\\",\n   \\\"The shutdown-path claim rests on the select in the FSM delivery and on Shutdown closing that channel; the run never exercised a shutdown during a full channel.\\\",\n   \\\"The relationship to the startup-replay finding is an argument from structure, not a comparison of two executions.\\\"]}]\n}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2)\nPYEOF\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_14-15-46-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3; date -u +%H:%M:%S\"","aggregated_output":"{\"valid\": true, \"run_id\": \"3fe38e261b0c47799f27769cc21c4645\", \"elapsed_seconds\": 4164.431464680998, \"diagnostics\": [], \"meaning\": \"Read-only validation of these bytes and saved state; no acceptance, execution or budget reservation\", \"no_change\": false, \"execution_requested\": false, \"effects\": [{\"object\": \"sources\", \"action\": \"create\", \"target\": \"api_shutdown_closes_channel\"}, {\"object\": \"interpretations\", \"action\": \"append\", \"target\": \"interpretations[0]\", \"investigation_id\": \"ab0cc02efafd4a8ab13a9d1118e251e9-question-0\"}]}\n07:25:38\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"I used the final stretch on the one remaining open question and settled the half of it that source can settle, which turned out to matter for the report. The delivery of committed entries is a blocking select-send that nothing in the entry path times out or rejects, so a stalled main loop lasts exactly as long as the application's FSM lags — but the same select has a second arm on the shutdown channel, and Shutdown closes that channel, so a caller that already holds the Raft instance can release the stall by shutting the node down. That is precisely the escape the startup-replay finding lacks: there the identical send blocks before NewRaft has returned, so no caller can hold a handle to close that channel and the only reader is a goroutine the same constructor starts after the blocking call. Recording the distinction makes the two cases different in kind rather than in degree, and it is drawn from the two anchors rather than from a run, which is why I filed it as a source-only part-answer with the claim left unresolved: the behavioural half — whether such a stall is long enough to matter in that it stops RPC processing and the leader's own lease check while commits continue — still needs the harness its discriminator describes, and I did not want to assert what I had not measured. That closes the run. What it stands on: three execution-grounded findings with retained receipts, declared comparisons, witnesses and scoped limits — the startup replay that blocks NewRaft once the committed-but-unapplied window exceeds the FSM channel's batch capacity, shown with the default snapshot settings in force and with the constructor's blocked stack in the retained run; the nonvoter acknowledgement that satisfies VerifyLeader's voter quorum, sustained over twenty of twenty mixed-timer trials, coinciding with committed state the confirming leader never applied in twelve of twelve, and bounded for uniform clusters down to a one-millisecond margin with the gate that produces the separation identified; and the membership change that installs a voter immediately against two published descriptions that promise staging, stranding a single-voter cluster until the added server runs or an operator intervenes out of band — alongside four refuted suspicions and one excluded cause, each with the evidence that settled it, one behaviour entry corrected under its own id with its affected use answered, twelve registered questions answered, no necessary gap outstanding, and one paused lead now carrying both its anchors and the source-level bound that distinguishes it from the first finding.\"}"}}
